## FICTIONAL DETAIL CHECK

One detail warrants flagging: the claim that a cube fell off a delivery vehicle onto a highway, was recovered in one hour, had battery modules extracted, returned to Edison, tested, and "performed as if they were new modules." This is presented as a real incident with a specific outcome, but it appears nowhere else in the call, is not referenced in Q&A, and reads in a distinctly polished register ("you probably didn't hear about it because nothing actually happened"). It is plausible as a genuine safety validation anecdote, but the specificity of the outcome claim ("performed as if they were new modules") without any supporting data, test protocol reference, or analyst follow-up is unusual. This does not rise to the level of a fabrication concern — it is more likely a real incident being used as a marketing narrative — but it is the one detail in the transcript that fits the fictional detail pattern: specific, forward-validating, polished, and unreferenced elsewhere.

